    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p><strong>Does under desk elliptical work?</strong> It depends entirely on what you want it to do. For some goals, such as moving more during a desk day or loosening stiff legs, it works quickly and reliably. For others, such as weight loss, it helps only when other habits line up. For a few, such as building visible muscle, it is the wrong tool.</p>
<p>This page goes goal by goal. For each one you get a plain verdict (works, partly or no), what to realistically expect, and how long it usually takes. If you want the broader research picture first, read our <a href="/does-an-under-desk-elliptical-work/">evidence-based answer on whether under desk ellipticals work</a>.</p>

<h2>Does Under Desk Elliptical Work? The Scorecard</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Goal</th><th>Verdict</th><th>Typical timeframe</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Weight loss</td><td>Partly</td><td>2 to 3 months, with diet</td></tr>
<tr><td>Toning legs</td><td>Partly</td><td>Firmer feel in 4 to 8 weeks; little visible change</td></tr>
<tr><td>Circulation</td><td>Partly</td><td>Lighter-feeling legs within days</td></tr>
<tr><td>Focus and energy</td><td>Partly</td><td>Same day for some people, not all</td></tr>
<tr><td>Stiffness and back comfort</td><td>Works (stiffness) / Partly (back)</td><td>1 to 2 weeks</td></tr>
<tr><td>Seniors' mobility</td><td>Works</td><td>2 to 6 weeks</td></tr>
<tr><td>Step count</td><td>No (not directly)</td><td>Needs a conversion</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Results assume regular use: most workdays, in blocks that add up to an hour or more. Occasional use gives occasional results.</p>

<h2>Weight Loss: Partly Works</h2>
<p>An under desk elliptical raises your calorie burn above sitting. At light to moderate effort, most people burn somewhere around 100 to 200 calories an hour in total, which is roughly 50 to 110 more than sitting still. Two hours a day on five days adds up to several hundred extra calories a week.</p>
<p>That is helpful, but it is not enough to cause weight loss if your eating stays the same or creeps up. Think of it as a way to widen a calorie deficit you are already creating with food.</p>
<p><strong>Timeframe:</strong> if your diet is in check, you may notice a difference on the scale in two to three months. Our guide on <a href="/can-you-lose-weight-with-under-desk-elliptical/">losing weight with an under desk elliptical</a> has a sample weekly plan.</p>

<h2>Toning: Partly Works</h2>
<p>Every stride works your quadriceps, hamstrings, glutes and calves. With moderate resistance, many owners say their legs feel firmer and less tired at the end of the day. That is a real improvement in muscular endurance.</p>
<p>What you should not expect is visible muscle definition or strength gains. Building muscle requires heavier loads than a compact seated machine provides. If toning is your main goal, pair the elliptical with squats, lunges or other strength work two or three times a week. Our breakdown of <a href="/what-muscles-does-an-under-desk-elliptical-work/">which muscles an under desk elliptical works</a> shows where you will feel it most.</p>
<p><strong>Timeframe:</strong> four to eight weeks for less leg fatigue; visible change mostly depends on your strength training.</p>

<h2>Circulation: Partly Works</h2>
<p>When you sit still, your calf muscles are mostly idle. Those muscles help push blood back up from your lower legs when they contract. Moving your ankles and calves while seated may help blood flow compared with sitting motionless, and many owners say their legs and feet feel less heavy by late afternoon.</p>
<p>Motorized machines such as the [[amazon:legxercise|LegXercise]] or [[amazon:ancheer|ANCHEER]] can provide this movement for people who cannot pedal much on their own.</p>
<div class="callout warn"><p>If you have a diagnosed circulation problem, swelling, a history of blood clots or diabetes-related foot issues, ask your doctor before using any leg exerciser. A machine is not a substitute for medical treatment.</p></div>
<p><strong>Timeframe:</strong> the lighter-legs feeling often shows up within the first few days.</p>

<h2>Focus and Energy: Partly Works</h2>
<p>This one varies the most between people. Some find that easy pedaling helps them stay alert through long reading, calls or video meetings, much like pacing helps some people think. Others find it distracting, especially during detailed work that needs both hands and full attention.</p>
<p>The trick is to match intensity to the task. Very light resistance during typing, a bit more during calls, and none at all during deep-focus work if it bothers you.</p>
<p><strong>Timeframe:</strong> you will know within a few days whether it helps or hinders you. Give yourself a week to adjust before deciding.</p>

<h2>Stiffness and Back Pain: Works for Stiffness, Partly for Back</h2>
<p>For stiff knees, hips and ankles after hours in a chair, gentle continuous movement is one of the most consistent benefits owners report. The elliptical path is smooth and low impact, which most people find easier on the knees than a desk bike's circular motion.</p>
<p>Back pain is more complicated. Some owners say moving their legs makes long sitting more comfortable and reduces the urge to slump. But pedaling can also pull you forward in your chair if the machine is too far away or the seat is too low. Set the machine so your knees stay below hip height at the top of each stride and keep your back against the chair.</p>
<div class="callout"><p><strong>Setup tip:</strong> a chair without wheels, or [[amazon:caster-cups|caster cups]] under a rolling chair, stops you drifting backward and hunching to reach the pedals.</p></div>
<p>If your back pain is persistent, sharp or radiates down your leg, see a doctor or physical therapist. An under desk elliptical is a comfort tool, not a treatment.</p>
<p><strong>Timeframe:</strong> less stiffness within one to two weeks of daily use.</p>

<h2>Seniors' Mobility: Works</h2>
<p>For older adults, an under desk elliptical can be one of the easiest ways to keep legs moving every day. You sit in a stable chair, so balance is not an issue, and the low-impact motion is gentle on arthritic joints. Many seniors use one while watching TV or reading.</p>
<p>Manual models such as the [[amazon:cubii-move|Cubii Move]] are light and simple. If pedaling against resistance is difficult, a motorized model moves the legs through the motion, and you can pedal along as strength improves. Our <a href="/best-under-desk-elliptical-for-seniors/">best under desk elliptical for seniors</a> guide compares the options.</p>
<p><strong>Timeframe:</strong> easier movement and more confidence often show up in two to six weeks. Check with a doctor before starting if you have heart, joint or circulation concerns.</p>

<h2>Step Count: Doesn't Work Directly</h2>
<p>If your goal is to hit a daily step number, an under desk elliptical will disappoint you on its own. Wrist trackers count steps from arm swing, and your arms stay still while you pedal. Some machines show a stride count on their display, but that is not the same as walking steps.</p>
<p>You can still convert your pedaling time into an approximate step-equivalent and log it as a workout. Try it here:</p>
[[tool:steps]]
<p>More detail is in <a href="/does-under-desk-elliptical-count-as-steps/">does an under desk elliptical count as steps</a>.</p>

<h2>Does Under Desk Elliptical Work Better With the Right Machine?</h2>
<p>Yes, partly. A machine that is quiet, smooth and fits under your desk is one you will keep using, and use matters more than anything else. Manual and motorized machines also suit different goals; we compare them in <a href="/do-under-desk-elliptical-machines/">do under desk elliptical machines work: manual vs motorized</a>.</p>
<p>Based on specs and owner reviews, these models fit the widest range of goals:</p>
